% TEMPLATE FOR REVISION LETTER
% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
% INSTRUCTIONS FOR USING THIS TEMPLATE:
% 
% 1.  Fill in the manuscript title in the \memosubject and in the body of the letter.
% 2.  Fill in the main body of the letter, including the overview of revisions
%     and responses to reviewers.
% 3.  Use the 'changes' package commands to highlight changes to your manuscript:
%     - \added{new text} for additions (will appear in green).
%     - \deleted{old text} for deletions (will appear in red with strikethrough).
%     - \replaced{new text}{old text} for replacements.
% 
% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\documentclass[a4paper,12pt]{texMemo}
\usepackage[english]{babel}
\usepackage{graphicx, lipsum}
\usepackage{todonotes}
\usepackage{amssymb} % diamond sign
\usepackage{changes}
\definecolor{mynewtext}{RGB}{0,180,0} % Define a custom color for new text (green)
\definecolor{myoldtext}{RGB}{255,0,0} % Define a custom color for old text (red)

\setdeletedmarkup{\textcolor{myoldtext}{\sout{#1}}} % Strike through with red for deleted text
\setaddedmarkup{\textcolor{mynewtext}{#1}} % Green for added text
\usepackage{standalone}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%refernce
%citation style and decoration
\usepackage{hyperref}
\hypersetup{
    colorlinks=true,
    linkcolor=red,  % Color for internal links (e.g., table of contents)
    urlcolor=blue,  % Color for URLs
    citecolor=blue % Color for citations (bibliography)
}
\usepackage{natbib} % or \usepackage{apalike}
% Customize the "et al." threshold for natbib
\setcitestyle{authoryear,round,longnamesfirst}
% Redefine \citep to include blue color
\let\oldcitep\citep
\renewcommand{\citep}[1]{\textcolor{blue}{\oldcitep{#1}}} %(10)
% \renewcommand{\citep}[1]{\textcolor{blue}{[\oldcitep{#1}]}} %[10]

\usepackage{setspace} % Add the setspace package
% \doublespacing % Set the line spacing to 2.0
\usepackage{amsmath}

\usepackage{times}
\memoto{Editor-in-Chief \ ISPRS Journal of Photogrammetry and Remote Sensing}
\memofrom{Getnet Demil \\ Mourad Oussalah}

\memosubject{\textbf{Submission of revised paper entitled "XFuse: Multi-Sensor CNN Transformer Fusion with Cross Attention and Quality Aware Curriculum Learning for High Resolution Fractional Snow Cover Mapping"}}
\memodate{\today}

\begin{document}
\maketitle
Dear Editor-in-Chief, \ 

We are writing to express our deep appreciation to you and the reviewers for the thorough and insightful feedback provided on our manuscript titled "\textbf{XFuse: Multi-Sensor CNN Transformer Fusion with Cross Attention and Quality Aware Curriculum Learning for High Resolution Fractional Snow Cover Mapping}". The comments were not only detailed but also demonstrated a strong commitment to enhancing the quality of our work, which we value immensely. We are truly grateful for the time and effort invested in the review process, and we have carefully considered and incorporated the suggestions provided.

We provide below the overview of our revision, followed by point-to-point response to editorial' and reviewers' suggestions/queries.
\todo[inline]{The detailed response to individual queries is highlighted in dark orange box.}  
In addition, we also enclose in this submission the track-to-track change document to enable reviewers / editorial track individual changes made in this manuscript. This highlights the \added{ new texts in green}, \replaced{replacement texts in green}{  old texts in red with strip}, and also \deleted{ deleted texts in red with strip}.

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
% OVERVIEW OF REVISIONS
%
% Provide a summary of the major changes made to the manuscript in response
% to the reviewers' feedback.
% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
\section*{Overview of Revisions}
In response to the constructive feedback, we have undertaken a comprehensive revision of our manuscript. Below, we outline the significant changes and improvements made:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Fixed Missing Citation (R2-6)}:
        \begin{itemize}
            \item Corrected the missing citation ``[? ]'' on Page 6, Section 2.1 by replacing the incorrect reference key \texttt{riggs2017overview} with the correct \texttt{riggs2017modis} for the VIIRS snow cover product documentation.
            \item \textit{Location: Section 2.1 (Related Work - Operational Snow Products), Line 8}
        \end{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Revised Abstract for Clarity (R1-1)}:
        \begin{itemize}
            \item Rewrote the abstract to be more concise (~250 words, reduced from ~350 words) with clear enumeration of three principal contributions.
            \item Restructured to lead with methodology and insights rather than exhaustive numerical results.
            \item \textit{Location: Abstract (Section 0)}
        \end{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Clarified Novelty in Methodology Section (R1-1, R1-2)}:
        \begin{itemize}
            \item Added a new ``Distinction from Prior Work'' paragraph in Section 3.1 (Overview) explicitly articulating what differentiates our approach from existing SAR-optical fusion and multi-resolution methods.
            \item Added clarifying text in Section 3.3 (Cross-Attention Fusion) explaining why bidirectional cross-attention differs from concatenation/ensemble approaches.
            \item Added clarifying text in Section 3.2 (Multi-Resolution Processing) distinguishing our adaptive aggregation from fixed pooling operations.
            \item Added clarifying text in Section 3.4 (Quality-Aware Learning) contrasting our approach with standard equal-weighted training and binary masking.
            \item \textit{Location: Section 3.1, 3.2, 3.3, 3.4}
        \end{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Clarified Evaluation Resolution Protocol (R2-1)}:
        \begin{itemize}
            \item Added new ``Evaluation Resolution and Protocol'' paragraph in Section 4.1 explicitly stating that all quantitative metrics are computed at 60m resolution.
            \item Clarified that input S1/S2 data are aggregated from 10m to 60m during training to match GFSC reference resolution.
            \item Explained that 10m prediction capability refers to inference-time guided disaggregation, validated qualitatively but not quantitatively.
            \item Expanded Limitation \#4 in Section 5.5 to acknowledge the lack of independent high-resolution ground truth validation.
            \item \textit{Location: Section 4.1, Section 5.5}
        \end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
% SPECIFIC REVIEWER COMMENTS
%
% Address the specific comments from each reviewer in a point-by-point manner.
% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
\section*{Response to Reviewer 1}

\textbf{R1-1: Writing clarity and core contributions not sufficiently distilled}
\begin{quote}
\textit{``In terms of writing, including the abstract and the methodology sections, the manuscript does not sufficiently distill and highlight the core contributions. Overall, it reads more like a technical report than a scientific paper, lacking clear summarization of the main ideas and insights.''}
\end{quote}

We thank the reviewer for this constructive feedback. We agree that the original manuscript was overly dense with technical details and numerical results, making it difficult to identify the core scientific contributions. We have substantially revised both the abstract and methodology sections to address this concern.

\textbf{Abstract Revision:} The original abstract (~350 words) was restructured and condensed to ~250 words. Key changes include:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Contribution-focused structure:} Instead of mixing problem description with exhaustive results, we now present three clearly enumerated contributions: (1) bidirectional cross-attention fusion, (2) multi-resolution training strategy ($10\text{m} \rightarrow 60\text{m} \rightarrow 10\text{m}$), and (3) quality-aware curriculum learning.
    \item \textbf{Reduced numerical density:} We retained only the most essential metrics (13.77\% MAE, 94.66\% accuracy, 38.7\% MAE reduction, ECE=0.025) rather than exhaustively listing all baseline comparisons and statistical tests.
    \item \textbf{Insight-first presentation:} The abstract now leads with the methodological insight (why cross-attention matters for SAR-optical fusion) before presenting results.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Methodology Section Revision:} We added a new ``Distinction from Prior Work'' paragraph in Section 3.1 (Overview) that explicitly articulates the scientific contributions before diving into technical details. This paragraph (now appearing after the general overview) states:

\begin{quote}
``While SAR-optical fusion and multi-resolution training have been explored independently in remote sensing literature, existing approaches face specific limitations that our methodology addresses. First, conventional fusion strategies (concatenation, late fusion, attention-weighted averaging) treat modalities symmetrically without explicitly modeling \textit{how} information from one sensor should inform interpretation of the other... Second, multi-resolution approaches typically employ fixed aggregation (average/max pooling) that ignores scene content... Third, existing quality-aware methods often use binary masks or single-source quality indicators... The novelty lies not in the individual concepts but in their principled integration within a unified architecture.''
\end{quote}

\textit{Changes in manuscript:} Abstract (complete rewrite with track changes); Section 3.1 (new ``Distinction from Prior Work'' paragraph added).

\textbf{R1-2: Novelty concerns regarding SAR-optical fusion and multi-resolution training}
\begin{quote}
\textit{``Regarding the proposed method, there already exist numerous studies that combine optical and SAR data for various applications. The core idea of this work does not appear to be particularly novel. In addition, the strategy of training with low-resolution data while leveraging high-resolution data has also been explored in related literature.''}
\end{quote}

We appreciate the reviewer raising this important point, which allows us to clarify the specific novelty of our approach. The reviewer is correct that SAR-optical fusion and coarse-to-fine training are established concepts. Our contribution lies not in these general ideas but in \textbf{specific design choices} that address limitations of existing implementations. We have added explicit clarifications throughout the methodology section:

\textbf{(1) Bidirectional Cross-Attention vs. Conventional Fusion (Section 3.3):}

Existing SAR-optical fusion approaches predominantly use:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textit{Early fusion (concatenation):} Treats modalities as additional channels, assuming equal relevance everywhere
    \item \textit{Late fusion (ensemble):} Combines independent predictions without cross-modal information exchange during feature learning
    \item \textit{Attention-weighted concatenation:} Learns importance weights but still treats the combination symmetrically
\end{itemize}

Our cross-attention mechanism is fundamentally different: it enables \textit{selective, asymmetric information exchange} where SAR features explicitly query optical features (and vice versa) through learned query-key-value projections. This is particularly important because the SAR-optical relationship is inherently asymmetric-SAR provides structural information useful when optical data is cloud-contaminated, while optical spectral signatures disambiguate SAR backscatter variations caused by moisture, roughness, or snow wetness.

We added the following clarification to Section 3.3:
\begin{quote}
``Unlike early fusion (concatenation) that treats modalities as additional channels, or late fusion (ensemble) that combines independent predictions, cross-attention enables \textit{selective information exchange}: each modality learns to query task-relevant features from the other. This is particularly important for SAR-optical fusion where the relationship is asymmetric...''
\end{quote}

\textbf{(2) Adaptive Aggregation vs. Fixed Pooling (Section 3.2):}

Existing multi-resolution approaches use fixed aggregation operations (average pooling, max pooling, or bilinear interpolation) applied uniformly regardless of scene content. Our adaptive aggregation learns to make content-aware decisions-using max pooling in snow-dominated regions to preserve peak signals and average pooling elsewhere to maintain spatial consistency.

We added the following clarification to Section 3.2:
\begin{quote}
``Unlike fixed pooling operations (average or max) that apply uniformly regardless of scene content, our aggregation adapts based on learned snow probability-using max pooling in snow-dominated regions to preserve peak signals and average pooling elsewhere to maintain spatial consistency. This content-aware design prevents the information loss that occurs when fixed aggregation averages out fine-scale snow boundaries.''
\end{quote}

\textbf{(3) Multi-Source Quality Integration vs. Binary Masking (Section 3.4):}

Existing quality-aware methods typically use binary cloud masks (include/exclude) or single-source quality indicators. Our approach integrates heterogeneous metadata from multiple independent sources (Sentinel-2 SCL, AOT, WVP; GFSC QC, QCFLAGS) through multiplicative weighting with curriculum scheduling.

We added the following clarification to Section 3.4:
\begin{quote}
``Conventional deep learning treats all training samples equally, assuming homogeneous data quality. This assumption is systematically violated in satellite imagery where cloud contamination, atmospheric interference, sensor artifacts, and reference data uncertainty vary spatially and temporally. Simple binary masking (excluding low-quality pixels) discards potentially useful information, while equal weighting allows noisy samples to corrupt learned representations. Our approach instead modulates the contribution of each pixel through continuous quality weights derived from multiple independent metadata sources, combined with curriculum scheduling that progressively introduces challenging samples as the model develops robustness.''
\end{quote}

\textbf{Summary:} The novelty lies in the \textit{specific design choices} within each component and their \textit{principled integration} for satellite-based snow monitoring, not in the general concepts of fusion or multi-resolution processing.

\textit{Changes in manuscript:} Section 3.1 (new ``Distinction from Prior Work'' paragraph); Section 3.2 (added clarification on adaptive vs. fixed aggregation); Section 3.3 (added clarification on cross-attention vs. concatenation/ensemble); Section 3.4 (added clarification on quality-aware training vs. standard approaches).

\textbf{R1-3: Missing cloud contamination experiments}
\begin{quote}
\textit{``The experimental results do not adequately substantiate or address the limitations of existing methods discussed in the introduction. In particular, scenarios such as cloud-covered conditions, which are critical for snow cover mapping and explicitly mentioned as challenges, are not properly investigated, and corresponding experiments are missing.''}
\end{quote}
We thank the reviewer for this important point. The reviewer is correct that cloud contamination is central to our motivation, and we agree that explicit experimental evidence was missing. We have addressed this through two complementary analyses, now presented in the new Section~4.6 (\textit{Robustness to Cloud Contamination}) of the revised manuscript.

\textbf{(1) Architectural robustness is built into the training design.}

Cloud robustness is not only a post-hoc property of our model, it is embedded in the training framework. Our quality-aware training (Section~3.4) incorporates Sentinel-2 SCL cloud masks as per-pixel loss weights: pixels classified as cloud-contaminated (SCL classes 7/8/9: low/medium/high probability cloud; class 10: thin cirrus; class 3: shadow) receive down-weighted loss contributions during training. This design was deliberate: the model is explicitly trained to leverage SAR structural information when optical signals are unreliable, rather than memorising optical artefacts. The SCL integration spans all 18,117 training patches, subjecting the model to heterogeneous cloud conditions during learning.

\textbf{(2) Controlled cloud occlusion experiment (new, Section~4.6).}

We conduct a controlled \textit{simulated cloud occlusion} experiment: all Sentinel-2 input channels are set to zero for every test patch, simulating complete optical loss and leaving Sentinel-1 SAR as the sole input modality. This controlled design cleanly isolates SAR's independent contribution without the confounds of partial cloud coverage or variable cloud types.

Results on all 18,117 test patches (new Table~5):

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{XFuse (S1+S2 full):} 91.9\% accuracy, 13.8\% MAE - full multi-modal performance.
    \item \textbf{XFuse (S2 zeroed, SAR only):} \textbf{83.2\% accuracy, 17.6\% MAE} - only 8.7 pp below full performance despite complete optical loss.
    \item \textbf{Early Fusion (S1+S2 full):} 81.3\% accuracy, 25.3\% MAE.
    \item \textbf{CNN-Only (S1+S2 full):} 87.5\% accuracy, 22.5\% MAE.
\end{itemize}

The key finding: \textbf{XFuse with no optical data (83.2\%) outperforms Early Fusion operating with its complete multi-modal input (81.3\%)}. This directly demonstrates that the bidirectional cross-attention mechanism internalises complementary SAR-optical representations. When S2 is unavailable, attention weights shift to prioritise S1 features, maintaining snow discrimination that exceeds concatenation-based baselines even at full capability. Optical baselines (Early Fusion, CNN-Only) have no analogous adaptive mechanism and cannot compensate for a missing modality.

We use a controlled occlusion design rather than relying on natural cloud variability because it provides a clean, reproducible test of SAR's independent contribution: natural cloud cover introduces confounding factors (varying cloud type, partial coverage, heterogeneous SCL labels) that make causal attribution difficult. The controlled experiment directly answers the question: \textit{what does SAR contribute when optical data is unavailable?}

\textit{Changes in manuscript:} New Section~4.6 (\textit{Robustness to Cloud Contamination}), including Table~3 with occlusion experiment results. Section~3.4 already describes the SCL-based quality-aware training but we have added an explicit connection to cloud robustness in the new section.

\textbf{R1-4: Insufficient qualitative visualizations}
\begin{quote}
\textit{``The results section lacks sufficient qualitative visualizations, making it difficult to intuitively evaluate the effectiveness and advantages of the proposed approach.''}
\end{quote}
\todo[inline]{Response to be added after new comparison figures are generated.}

