\textbf{R1-5: Figure clarity issues}
\begin{quote}
\textit{``Many figures are unclear, with text and details that are difficult to read.''}
\end{quote}

We thank the reviewer for this feedback. We have addressed figure clarity through targeted redesigns and, in one case, a format conversion that better serves the reader.

\textbf{Figure 2 (Architecture overview):} The original diagram contained dense annotation layers with overlapping labels and small font sizes that made individual components difficult to distinguish at typical print resolution. The revised Figure~2 adopts a cleaner layout with increased inter-component spacing, larger and consistently sized label fonts, clearly delineated module boundaries, and a simplified color scheme that reduces visual clutter while preserving all architectural information. The dual-path CNN blocks, cross-attention bridge, transformer encoder, and resolution-aggregation stages are each rendered as self-contained visual units with unambiguous data-flow arrows.

\textbf{Figure 4 (Cross-attention fusion detail):} The original figure used a complex multi-panel layout where the query-key-value projections and the bidirectional attention paths were difficult to trace. The revised Figure~4 simplifies the presentation to a single, linearly ordered diagram that follows the forward-pass sequence: (i) modality-specific feature maps, (ii) Q/K/V projection heads for each direction ($\text{S1} \rightarrow \text{S2}$ and $\text{S2} \rightarrow \text{S1}$), (iii) scaled dot-product attention, and (iv) fused output concatenation. Font sizes and arrow weights have been standardized to ensure legibility at both screen and print resolutions.

\textbf{Figure 5 (Quality-aware training framework):} The original PNG diagram attempted to convey a multi-step algorithmic procedure---curriculum threshold scheduling, per-pixel quality weighting, uncertainty loss, and optimizer update---within a single flowchart image. At journal column width this resulted in text that was too small to read without magnification. We have replaced this figure entirely with a formal \textbf{Algorithm~1} pseudo-code block (using the standard \texttt{algorithmic} environment), which is the conventional and more readable format for presenting training procedures in machine-learning papers. The algorithm explicitly enumerates all steps: curriculum threshold decay (line~4), mini-batch filtering (line~6), forward pass and uncertainty head (lines~7--8), confidence and quality weight computation (lines~9--10), the quality-aware and uncertainty losses (lines~11--12), the multi-component total loss (line~13), and the AdamW update (line~14). Key performance indicators (ECE\,=\,0.025; Moran's\,I\,=\,0.548, $p=0.002$) appear as a final comment on the \textbf{return} statement so the reader can immediately connect the procedure to its empirical outcome.

\textit{Changes in manuscript:} Figures~2 and~4 redesigned for clarity (larger fonts, simplified layouts, consistent color coding); Figure~5 replaced by Algorithm~1 (pseudo-code block).

