% TEMPLATE FOR REVISION LETTER
% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
% INSTRUCTIONS FOR USING THIS TEMPLATE:
% 
% 1.  Fill in the manuscript title in the \memosubject and in the body of the letter.
% 2.  Fill in the main body of the letter, including the overview of revisions
%     and responses to reviewers.
% 3.  Use the 'changes' package commands to highlight changes to your manuscript:
%     - \added{new text} for additions (will appear in green).
%     - \deleted{old text} for deletions (will appear in red with strikethrough).
%     - \replaced{new text}{old text} for replacements.
% 
% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\documentclass[a4paper,12pt]{texMemo}
\usepackage[english]{babel}
\usepackage{graphicx, lipsum}
\usepackage{todonotes}
\usepackage{amssymb} % diamond sign
\usepackage{changes}
\definecolor{mynewtext}{RGB}{0,180,0} % Define a custom color for new text (green)
\definecolor{myoldtext}{RGB}{255,0,0} % Define a custom color for old text (red)

\setdeletedmarkup{\textcolor{myoldtext}{\sout{#1}}} % Strike through with red for deleted text
\setaddedmarkup{\textcolor{mynewtext}{#1}} % Green for added text
\usepackage{standalone}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%refernce
%citation style and decoration
\usepackage{hyperref}
\hypersetup{
    colorlinks=true,
    linkcolor=red,  % Color for internal links (e.g., table of contents)
    urlcolor=blue,  % Color for URLs
    citecolor=blue % Color for citations (bibliography)
}
\usepackage{natbib} % or \usepackage{apalike}
% Customize the "et al." threshold for natbib
\setcitestyle{authoryear,round,longnamesfirst}
% Redefine \citep to include blue color
\let\oldcitep\citep
\renewcommand{\citep}[1]{\textcolor{blue}{\oldcitep{#1}}} %(10)
% \renewcommand{\citep}[1]{\textcolor{blue}{[\oldcitep{#1}]}} %[10]

\usepackage{setspace} % Add the setspace package
% \doublespacing % Set the line spacing to 2.0
\usepackage{amsmath}

\usepackage{times}
\memoto{Editor-in-Chief \ ISPRS Journal of Photogrammetry and Remote Sensing}
\memofrom{Getnet Demil \\ Mourad Oussalah}

\memosubject{\textbf{Submission of revised paper entitled "XFuse: Multi-Sensor CNN Transformer Fusion with Cross Attention and Quality Aware Curriculum Learning for High Resolution Fractional Snow Cover Mapping"}}
\memodate{\today}

\begin{document}
\maketitle
Dear Editor-in-Chief, \ 

We are writing to express our deep appreciation to you and the reviewers for the thorough and insightful feedback provided on our manuscript titled "\textbf{XFuse: Multi-Sensor CNN Transformer Fusion with Cross Attention and Quality Aware Curriculum Learning for High Resolution Fractional Snow Cover Mapping}". The comments were not only detailed but also demonstrated a strong commitment to enhancing the quality of our work, which we value immensely. We are truly grateful for the time and effort invested in the review process, and we have carefully considered and incorporated the suggestions provided.

We provide below the overview of our revision, followed by point-to-point response to editorial' and reviewers' suggestions/queries.
\todo[inline]{The detailed response to individual queries is highlighted in dark orange box.}  
In addition, we also enclose in this submission the track-to-track change document to enable reviewers / editorial track individual changes made in this manuscript. This highlights the \added{ new texts in green}, \replaced{replacement texts in green}{  old texts in red with strip}, and also \deleted{ deleted texts in red with strip}.

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
% OVERVIEW OF REVISIONS
%
% Provide a summary of the major changes made to the manuscript in response
% to the reviewers' feedback.
% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
\section*{Overview of Revisions}
In response to the constructive feedback, we have undertaken a comprehensive revision of our manuscript. Below, we outline the significant changes and improvements made:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Fixed Missing Citation (R2-6)}:
        \begin{itemize}
            \item Corrected the missing citation ``[? ]'' on Page 6, Section 2.1 by replacing the incorrect reference key \texttt{riggs2017overview} with the correct \texttt{riggs2017modis} for the VIIRS snow cover product documentation.
            \item \textit{Location: Section 2.1 (Related Work - Operational Snow Products), Line 8}
        \end{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Revised Abstract for Clarity (R1-1)}:
        \begin{itemize}
            \item Rewrote the abstract to be more concise (~250 words, reduced from ~350 words) with clear enumeration of three principal contributions.
            \item Restructured to lead with methodology and insights rather than exhaustive numerical results.
            \item \textit{Location: Abstract (Section 0)}
        \end{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Clarified Novelty in Methodology Section (R1-1, R1-2)}:
        \begin{itemize}
            \item Added a new ``Distinction from Prior Work'' paragraph in Section 3.1 (Overview) explicitly articulating what differentiates our approach from existing SAR-optical fusion and multi-resolution methods.
            \item Added clarifying text in Section 3.3 (Cross-Attention Fusion) explaining why bidirectional cross-attention differs from concatenation/ensemble approaches.
            \item Added clarifying text in Section 3.2 (Multi-Resolution Processing) distinguishing our adaptive aggregation from fixed pooling operations.
            \item Added clarifying text in Section 3.4 (Quality-Aware Learning) contrasting our approach with standard equal-weighted training and binary masking.
            \item \textit{Location: Section 3.1, 3.2, 3.3, 3.4}
        \end{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Clarified Evaluation Resolution Protocol (R2-1)}:
        \begin{itemize}
            \item Added new ``Evaluation Resolution and Protocol'' paragraph in Section 4.1 explicitly stating that all quantitative metrics are computed at 60m resolution.
            \item Clarified that input S1/S2 data are aggregated from 10m to 60m during training to match GFSC reference resolution.
            \item Explained that 10m prediction capability refers to inference-time guided disaggregation, validated qualitatively but not quantitatively.
            \item Expanded Limitation \#4 in Section 5.5 to acknowledge the lack of independent high-resolution ground truth validation.
            \item \textit{Location: Section 4.1, Section 5.5}
        \end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}

% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
% SPECIFIC REVIEWER COMMENTS
%
% Address the specific comments from each reviewer in a point-by-point manner.
% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
\section*{Response to Reviewer 1}

\textbf{R1-1: Writing clarity and core contributions not sufficiently distilled}
\begin{quote}
\textit{``In terms of writing, including the abstract and the methodology sections, the manuscript does not sufficiently distill and highlight the core contributions. Overall, it reads more like a technical report than a scientific paper, lacking clear summarization of the main ideas and insights.''}
\end{quote}

We thank the reviewer for this constructive feedback. We agree that the original manuscript was overly dense with technical details and numerical results, making it difficult to identify the core scientific contributions. We have substantially revised both the abstract and methodology sections to address this concern.

\textbf{Abstract Revision:} The original abstract (~350 words) was restructured and condensed to ~250 words. Key changes include:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Contribution-focused structure:} Instead of mixing problem description with exhaustive results, we now present three clearly enumerated contributions: (1) bidirectional cross-attention fusion, (2) multi-resolution training strategy ($10\text{m} \rightarrow 60\text{m} \rightarrow 10\text{m}$), and (3) quality-aware curriculum learning.
    \item \textbf{Reduced numerical density:} We retained only the most essential metrics (13.77\% MAE, 94.66\% accuracy, 38.7\% MAE reduction, ECE=0.025) rather than exhaustively listing all baseline comparisons and statistical tests.
    \item \textbf{Insight-first presentation:} The abstract now leads with the methodological insight (why cross-attention matters for SAR-optical fusion) before presenting results.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Methodology Section Revision:} We added a new ``Distinction from Prior Work'' paragraph in Section 3.1 (Overview) that explicitly articulates the scientific contributions before diving into technical details. This paragraph (now appearing after the general overview) states:

\begin{quote}
``While SAR-optical fusion and multi-resolution training have been explored independently in remote sensing literature, existing approaches face specific limitations that our methodology addresses. First, conventional fusion strategies (concatenation, late fusion, attention-weighted averaging) treat modalities symmetrically without explicitly modeling \textit{how} information from one sensor should inform interpretation of the other... Second, multi-resolution approaches typically employ fixed aggregation (average/max pooling) that ignores scene content... Third, existing quality-aware methods often use binary masks or single-source quality indicators... The novelty lies not in the individual concepts but in their principled integration within a unified architecture.''
\end{quote}

\textit{Changes in manuscript:} Abstract (complete rewrite with track changes); Section 3.1 (new ``Distinction from Prior Work'' paragraph added).

\textbf{R1-2: Novelty concerns regarding SAR-optical fusion and multi-resolution training}
\begin{quote}
\textit{``Regarding the proposed method, there already exist numerous studies that combine optical and SAR data for various applications. The core idea of this work does not appear to be particularly novel. In addition, the strategy of training with low-resolution data while leveraging high-resolution data has also been explored in related literature.''}
\end{quote}

We appreciate the reviewer raising this important point, which allows us to clarify the specific novelty of our approach. The reviewer is correct that SAR-optical fusion and coarse-to-fine training are established concepts. Our contribution lies not in these general ideas but in \textbf{specific design choices} that address limitations of existing implementations. We have added explicit clarifications throughout the methodology section:

\textbf{(1) Bidirectional Cross-Attention vs. Conventional Fusion (Section 3.3):}

Existing SAR-optical fusion approaches predominantly use:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textit{Early fusion (concatenation):} Treats modalities as additional channels, assuming equal relevance everywhere
    \item \textit{Late fusion (ensemble):} Combines independent predictions without cross-modal information exchange during feature learning
    \item \textit{Attention-weighted concatenation:} Learns importance weights but still treats the combination symmetrically
\end{itemize}

Our cross-attention mechanism is fundamentally different: it enables \textit{selective, asymmetric information exchange} where SAR features explicitly query optical features (and vice versa) through learned query-key-value projections. This is particularly important because the SAR-optical relationship is inherently asymmetric-SAR provides structural information useful when optical data is cloud-contaminated, while optical spectral signatures disambiguate SAR backscatter variations caused by moisture, roughness, or snow wetness.

We added the following clarification to Section 3.3:
\begin{quote}
``Unlike early fusion (concatenation) that treats modalities as additional channels, or late fusion (ensemble) that combines independent predictions, cross-attention enables \textit{selective information exchange}: each modality learns to query task-relevant features from the other. This is particularly important for SAR-optical fusion where the relationship is asymmetric...''
\end{quote}

\textbf{(2) Adaptive Aggregation vs. Fixed Pooling (Section 3.2):}

Existing multi-resolution approaches use fixed aggregation operations (average pooling, max pooling, or bilinear interpolation) applied uniformly regardless of scene content. Our adaptive aggregation learns to make content-aware decisions-using max pooling in snow-dominated regions to preserve peak signals and average pooling elsewhere to maintain spatial consistency.

We added the following clarification to Section 3.2:
\begin{quote}
``Unlike fixed pooling operations (average or max) that apply uniformly regardless of scene content, our aggregation adapts based on learned snow probability-using max pooling in snow-dominated regions to preserve peak signals and average pooling elsewhere to maintain spatial consistency. This content-aware design prevents the information loss that occurs when fixed aggregation averages out fine-scale snow boundaries.''
\end{quote}

\textbf{(3) Multi-Source Quality Integration vs. Binary Masking (Section 3.4):}

Existing quality-aware methods typically use binary cloud masks (include/exclude) or single-source quality indicators. Our approach integrates heterogeneous metadata from multiple independent sources (Sentinel-2 SCL, AOT, WVP; GFSC QC, QCFLAGS) through multiplicative weighting with curriculum scheduling.

We added the following clarification to Section 3.4:
\begin{quote}
``Conventional deep learning treats all training samples equally, assuming homogeneous data quality. This assumption is systematically violated in satellite imagery where cloud contamination, atmospheric interference, sensor artifacts, and reference data uncertainty vary spatially and temporally. Simple binary masking (excluding low-quality pixels) discards potentially useful information, while equal weighting allows noisy samples to corrupt learned representations. Our approach instead modulates the contribution of each pixel through continuous quality weights derived from multiple independent metadata sources, combined with curriculum scheduling that progressively introduces challenging samples as the model develops robustness.''
\end{quote}

\textbf{Summary:} The novelty lies in the \textit{specific design choices} within each component and their \textit{principled integration} for satellite-based snow monitoring, not in the general concepts of fusion or multi-resolution processing.

\textit{Changes in manuscript:} Section 3.1 (new ``Distinction from Prior Work'' paragraph); Section 3.2 (added clarification on adaptive vs. fixed aggregation); Section 3.3 (added clarification on cross-attention vs. concatenation/ensemble); Section 3.4 (added clarification on quality-aware training vs. standard approaches).

\textbf{R1-3: Missing cloud contamination experiments}
\begin{quote}
\textit{``The experimental results do not adequately substantiate or address the limitations of existing methods discussed in the introduction. In particular, scenarios such as cloud-covered conditions, which are critical for snow cover mapping and explicitly mentioned as challenges, are not properly investigated, and corresponding experiments are missing.''}
\end{quote}
We thank the reviewer for this important point. The reviewer is correct that cloud contamination is central to our motivation, and we agree that explicit experimental evidence was missing. We have addressed this through two complementary analyses, now presented in the new Section~4.6 (\textit{Robustness to Cloud Contamination}) of the revised manuscript.

\textbf{(1) Architectural robustness is built into the training design.}

Cloud robustness is not only a post-hoc property of our model, it is embedded in the training framework. Our quality-aware training (Section~3.4) incorporates Sentinel-2 SCL cloud masks as per-pixel loss weights: pixels classified as cloud-contaminated (SCL classes 7/8/9: low/medium/high probability cloud; class 10: thin cirrus; class 3: shadow) receive down-weighted loss contributions during training. This design was deliberate: the model is explicitly trained to leverage SAR structural information when optical signals are unreliable, rather than memorising optical artefacts. The SCL integration spans all 18,117 training patches, subjecting the model to heterogeneous cloud conditions during learning.

\textbf{(2) Controlled cloud occlusion experiment (new, Section~4.6).}

We conduct a controlled \textit{simulated cloud occlusion} experiment: all Sentinel-2 input channels are set to zero for every test patch, simulating complete optical loss and leaving Sentinel-1 SAR as the sole input modality. This controlled design cleanly isolates SAR's independent contribution without the confounds of partial cloud coverage or variable cloud types.

Results on all 18,117 test patches (new Table~5):

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{XFuse (S1+S2 full):} 91.9\% accuracy, 13.8\% MAE - full multi-modal performance.
    \item \textbf{XFuse (S2 zeroed, SAR only):} \textbf{83.2\% accuracy, 17.6\% MAE} - only 8.7 pp below full performance despite complete optical loss.
    \item \textbf{Early Fusion (S1+S2 full):} 81.3\% accuracy, 25.3\% MAE.
    \item \textbf{CNN-Only (S1+S2 full):} 87.5\% accuracy, 22.5\% MAE.
\end{itemize}

The key finding: \textbf{XFuse with no optical data (83.2\%) outperforms Early Fusion operating with its complete multi-modal input (81.3\%)}. This directly demonstrates that the bidirectional cross-attention mechanism internalises complementary SAR-optical representations. When S2 is unavailable, attention weights shift to prioritise S1 features, maintaining snow discrimination that exceeds concatenation-based baselines even at full capability. Optical baselines (Early Fusion, CNN-Only) have no analogous adaptive mechanism and cannot compensate for a missing modality.

We use a controlled occlusion design rather than relying on natural cloud variability because it provides a clean, reproducible test of SAR's independent contribution: natural cloud cover introduces confounding factors (varying cloud type, partial coverage, heterogeneous SCL labels) that make causal attribution difficult. The controlled experiment directly answers the question: \textit{what does SAR contribute when optical data is unavailable?}

\textit{Changes in manuscript:} New Section~4.6 (\textit{Robustness to Cloud Contamination}), including Table~3 with occlusion experiment results. Section~3.4 already describes the SCL-based quality-aware training but we have added an explicit connection to cloud robustness in the new section.

\textbf{R1-4: Insufficient qualitative visualizations}
\begin{quote}
\textit{``The results section lacks sufficient qualitative visualizations, making it difficult to intuitively evaluate the effectiveness and advantages of the proposed approach.''}
\end{quote}
\todo[inline]{Response to be added after new comparison figures are generated.}

\textbf{R1-5: Figure clarity issues}
\begin{quote}
\textit{``Many figures are unclear, with text and details that are difficult to read.''}
\end{quote}

We thank the reviewer for this feedback. We have addressed figure clarity through targeted redesigns and, in one case, a format conversion that better serves the reader.

\textbf{Figure 2 (Architecture overview):} The original diagram contained dense annotation layers with overlapping labels and small font sizes that made individual components difficult to distinguish at typical print resolution. The revised Figure~2 adopts a cleaner layout with increased inter-component spacing, larger and consistently sized label fonts, clearly delineated module boundaries, and a simplified color scheme that reduces visual clutter while preserving all architectural information. The dual-path CNN blocks, cross-attention bridge, transformer encoder, and resolution-aggregation stages are each rendered as self-contained visual units with unambiguous data-flow arrows.

\textbf{Figure 4 (Cross-attention fusion detail):} The original figure used a complex multi-panel layout where the query-key-value projections and the bidirectional attention paths were difficult to trace. The revised Figure~4 simplifies the presentation to a single, linearly ordered diagram that follows the forward-pass sequence: (i) modality-specific feature maps, (ii) Q/K/V projection heads for each direction ($\text{S1} \rightarrow \text{S2}$ and $\text{S2} \rightarrow \text{S1}$), (iii) scaled dot-product attention, and (iv) fused output concatenation. Font sizes and arrow weights have been standardized to ensure legibility at both screen and print resolutions.

\textbf{Figure 5 (Quality-aware training framework):} The original PNG diagram attempted to convey a multi-step algorithmic procedure---curriculum threshold scheduling, per-pixel quality weighting, uncertainty loss, and optimizer update---within a single flowchart image. At journal column width this resulted in text that was too small to read without magnification. We have replaced this figure entirely with a formal \textbf{Algorithm~1} pseudo-code block (using the standard \texttt{algorithmic} environment), which is the conventional and more readable format for presenting training procedures in machine-learning papers. The algorithm explicitly enumerates all steps: curriculum threshold decay (line~4), mini-batch filtering (line~6), forward pass and uncertainty head (lines~7--8), confidence and quality weight computation (lines~9--10), the quality-aware and uncertainty losses (lines~11--12), the multi-component total loss (line~13), and the AdamW update (line~14). Key performance indicators (ECE\,=\,0.025; Moran's\,I\,=\,0.548, $p=0.002$) appear as a final comment on the \textbf{return} statement so the reader can immediately connect the procedure to its empirical outcome.

\textit{Changes in manuscript:} Figures~2 and~4 redesigned for clarity (larger fonts, simplified layouts, consistent color coding); Figure~5 replaced by Algorithm~1 (pseudo-code block).

\section*{Response to Reviewer 2}

\textbf{R2-1: Evaluation protocol for 10m predictions against 60m ground truth}
\begin{quote}
\textit{``Since the ground truth (GFSC) is 60 m, how are the 10 m predictions evaluated quantitatively? Did the authors downsample their 10m predictions back to 60 m to calculate MAE against the GFSC labels? Or did they upsample the GFSC labels to 10m? [...] The authors should ideally provide a small subset of `true' high-resolution ground truth to demonstrate that the model actually recovers 10 m details and not just artifacts.''}
\end{quote}

We thank the reviewer for raising this important methodological question, which deserves explicit clarification. We have added a new paragraph titled ``Evaluation Resolution and Protocol'' in Section 4.1 to address this concern directly.

\textbf{Answer to the specific questions:}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{How are predictions evaluated?} The model is trained and evaluated entirely at \textbf{60m resolution}. We do \textit{not} downsample 10m predictions to 60m, nor do we upsample GFSC labels to 10m.

    \item \textbf{The actual workflow:}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 inputs at native 10m resolution are \textbf{aggregated to 60m} through mean pooling over $6 \times 6$ pixel blocks before being fed to the model
        \item The model predicts fractional snow cover at 60m resolution
        \item All metrics (MAE, RMSE, accuracy, $R^2$, etc.) are computed at 60m against GFSC labels
        \item This is implemented in our codebase: \texttt{aggregate\_10m\_to\_60m()} function applies mean aggregation over $6 \times 6$ blocks (e.g., 100$\times$100 pixels at 10m $\rightarrow$ 16$\times$16 pixels at 60m)
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{What about the ``10m prediction'' claim?} The multi-resolution framework ($10\text{m} \rightarrow 60\text{m} \rightarrow 10\text{m}$) enables optional \textit{inference-time} disaggregation from 60m predictions back to 10m through guided upsampling that leverages high-resolution input features. This is a capability for operational deployment, not part of the training/validation pipeline.
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Clarification added to manuscript:}

We have added the following explicit statement in Section 4.1 (Experimental Setup):

\begin{quote}
``During training, Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 inputs at native 10m resolution are aggregated to 60m through mean pooling over $6 \times 6$ pixel blocks to match the GFSC reference data resolution. The model predicts fractional snow cover at 60m resolution, and \textbf{all quantitative metrics reported in this paper are computed at 60m resolution} against GFSC labels. [...] However, we emphasize that the 10m outputs represent \textit{spatially enhanced predictions} guided by fine-scale input structure, not independently validated high-resolution products.''
\end{quote}

\textbf{Regarding high-resolution ground truth:}

The reviewer correctly notes that ideally we would validate 10m predictions against true high-resolution ground truth. We acknowledge this limitation explicitly:

\begin{itemize}
    \item High-resolution ($<$60m) fractional snow cover ground truth is not widely available for our study region
    \item The 10m predictions are validated \textit{qualitatively} by visual alignment with Sentinel-2 optical imagery boundaries (Figure 8 in the manuscript shows boundary preservation)
    \item \textit{Quantitative} validation of sub-60m accuracy would require independent high-resolution ground truth from UAV surveys, airborne sensors, or Landsat-derived 30m products
    \item We have added this as an explicit limitation and future work direction in Section 5.5
\end{itemize}

We believe this transparent clarification addresses the reviewer's concern. The methodology is scientifically sound: training at the resolution of available ground truth is standard practice in remote sensing, and the guided disaggregation capability provides operational value even without high-resolution validation.

\textit{Changes in manuscript:} Section 4.1 (new ``Evaluation Resolution and Protocol'' paragraph); Section 5.5 Limitation \#4 (expanded to clarify evaluation resolution).

\textbf{R2-2: Geographic generalization concerns (alpine terrain)}
\begin{quote}
\textit{``The study focuses on 29 tiles in Northern Finland. [...] How does XFuse handle complex alpine terrain (Alps or Himalayas) where SAR geometric distortions are severe? The current dataset might not sufficiently challenge the cross-attention fusion mechanism's ability to handle missing SAR information due to shadows.''}
\end{quote}
We thank the reviewer for raising this critical question, which addresses perhaps the most significant limitation of our current evaluation. The reviewer is entirely correct: northern Finland's boreal landscape represents a favorable scenario for SAR-based fusion, and we agree that geographic transferability is the primary open question for our methodology. We have added a substantial new ``Geographic Transferability'' paragraph as Limitation \#2 in Section~5.5 that addresses each of the reviewer's specific concerns in detail.

\textbf{(1) SAR Geometric Distortions in Alpine Terrain:}

Northern Finland's gentle topography (typical slopes $<$5\textdegree) effectively minimizes the geometric distortions that plague SAR observations in mountainous regions. In alpine terrain, the interaction between Sentinel-1's nominal incidence angles (29\textdegree--46\textdegree) and steep slopes produces three distinct degradation mechanisms:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textit{Foreshortening:} On foreslopes exceeding $\sim$20\textdegree, topographic relief is compressed in the range direction, degrading spatial resolution and mixing returns from different elevations.
    \item \textit{Layover:} When slope angle approaches the complement of the incidence angle, returns from multiple ground positions collapse into the same range cell, producing physically uninterpretable pixels.
    \item \textit{Radar shadow:} Steep back-slopes receive no radar illumination, creating complete signal voids.
\end{itemize}
These effects concentrate in precisely the high-altitude accumulation zones where snow monitoring is most critical for water resource management. Our results should therefore be interpreted as achievable under near-optimal SAR acquisition geometry.

\textbf{(2) Snow Metamorphism and Regime-Dependent SAR Signatures:}

The reviewer correctly identifies that SAR-snow interactions are highly dependent on snow type. Our study area's predominantly dry continental snow exhibits relatively stable C-band backscatter. In contrast:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textit{Maritime snowpacks} (warm, dense, frequent melt--freeze) produce strong C-band absorption through liquid water content, causing dramatic backscatter changes during diurnal cycles.
    \item \textit{Continental interior snowpacks} (cold, dry, faceted grains) exhibit volume scattering from depth hoar layers.
    \item \textit{Transitional regimes} (e.g., Himalayan basins) display complex elevation-dependent diurnal stratification.
\end{itemize}
Feature representations learned from boreal Finland may not generalize to these different physical regimes without recalibration or domain adaptation.

\textbf{(3) How the Architecture Could Address These Challenges:}

While we have not validated XFuse in alpine terrain, two architectural design choices are directly relevant to the reviewer's concern about ``handling missing SAR information'':
\begin{itemize}
    \item The \textit{bidirectional cross-attention mechanism} enables each modality to selectively attend to the complementary modality through learned query-key-value projections. In SAR-degraded regions (shadow, layover), the cross-attention weights could theoretically shift toward optical spectral features-analogous to the mechanism's designed behavior under cloud contamination, but operating in the reverse modality direction. The asymmetric nature of cross-attention (each modality independently queries the other) is specifically designed for scenarios where one sensor provides degraded information.
    \item The \textit{quality-aware training framework} already learns continuous sample weighting from heterogeneous metadata sources (SCL, AOT, WVP, QC flags). This framework provides a natural extension point: terrain-derived quality indicators-local incidence angle, DEM-based layover/shadow masks-could be incorporated as additional metadata channels, enabling the model to systematically down-weight geometrically distorted SAR observations during training.
\end{itemize}
However, we are careful to present these as \textit{architectural affordances} rather than demonstrated capabilities. Without validation on mountainous datasets, we cannot claim that the cross-attention mechanism will successfully adapt to SAR shadow regions in practice.

\textbf{(4) Concrete Path Forward:}

We identify four requirements for robust geographic generalization in the revised manuscript:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Radiometric terrain correction of Sentinel-1 data using high-resolution DEMs
    \item Incorporation of topographic variables (slope, aspect, curvature, sky view factor) as auxiliary inputs
    \item Training on geographically diverse datasets spanning boreal, alpine, and maritime snow regimes
    \item Domain adaptation techniques for efficient transfer to undersampled snow climate zones
\end{enumerate}
We frame multi-region validation as the primary direction for future work and state explicitly that our current results represent performance under favorable SAR conditions.

\textit{Changes in manuscript:} Section~5.5 (new Limitation \#2: ``Geographic Transferability''-comprehensive discussion of SAR geometric distortions in alpine terrain, snow metamorphism regime effects, architectural mitigation properties, and concrete future work requirements).

\textbf{R2-3: Baseline comparison fairness (multi-resolution head)}
\begin{quote}
\textit{``Are these baselines also equipped with the Multi-Resolution Processing (10m$\to$60m$\to$10m) head? If XFuse has the advantage of the guided upsampling head while baselines simply interpolate 60m predictions, the comparison is unfair.''}
\end{quote}
We appreciate this important question regarding experimental fairness. We confirm that \textbf{all baselines are trained and evaluated under identical conditions}, ensuring a fair comparison:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Identical data pipeline:} All models-Early Fusion, CNN-Only, Transformer-Only, and XFuse-use the same dataset class (\texttt{EnhancedDualResolutionDataset} with \\ \texttt{train\_mode=True}), which aggregates Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 inputs from native 10m resolution to 60m via mean pooling over $6 \times 6$ pixel blocks. No model receives higher-resolution inputs than any other.
    \item \textbf{Identical training targets:} All models predict against the same 60m GFSC reference labels. All quantitative metrics (MAE, RMSE, accuracy, F1, $R^2$) are computed at 60m resolution.
    \item \textbf{Multi-resolution head is inference-only:} The guided disaggregation ($60\text{m} \rightarrow 10\text{m}$) described in Section~3.2 is a post-processing step applied \textit{after} the core prediction during inference. It is \textbf{not} used during training or quantitative evaluation, and therefore does not contribute to any reported metric.
    \item \textbf{Ablation evidence confirms architectural gains:} The ablation study (Table~3) shows that removing multi-resolution processing reduces accuracy by only 1.76 percentage points (94.66\% $\rightarrow$ 92.90\%). Critically, XFuse \textit{without} multi-resolution processing (92.90\%) still outperforms the best baseline (CNN-Only: 89.13\%) by 3.77 percentage points, confirming that the performance gains are attributable to the cross-attention fusion and transformer components, not the resolution strategy.
\end{itemize}

\textit{Changes in manuscript:} Added explicit fairness clarification paragraph in Section~4.2; added isolation sentence in the ablation discussion (Section~4.4).

\textbf{R2-4: Data acquisition dates for reproducibility}
\begin{quote}
\textit{``The paper mentions Northern Finland during winter 2025 and citations include 2025 papers. [...] The authors should ensure the specific dates of acquisition are listed to ensure reproducibility.''}
\end{quote}
We thank the reviewer for this important reproducibility concern. We have added Table~2 (Section~4.1) with a full dataset summary, and we correct a labelling error in Sections~4.4 and~6 where the manuscript erroneously referred to ``winter 2025''; the dataset covers \textbf{December 2024 -- July 2025}, spanning the snow-free summer through the autumn snow-onset transition in northern Lapland. We apologise for this confusion.

\textbf{Dataset provenance (three-stage pipeline).} The data collection proceeded in three stages, and we describe all three to give the reviewer the full picture:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Raw archive.} We downloaded a raw archive of \textbf{178 co-registered Sentinel-1/Sentinel-2 acquisition pairs} (407\,GB total) from the ESA Copernicus Data Space Ecosystem (\url{https://dataspace.copernicus.eu}), spanning \textbf{December 2024 to July 2025} across \textbf{45 MGRS tiles} in Finland (60--70.5\textdegree{}N, 20--32\textdegree{}E, UTM zones 33--36), with a Sentinel-2 maximum cloud coverage filter of 20\% applied at retrieval time.

  \item \textbf{Quality-controlled preprocessing.} After radiometric calibration, terrain correction (SAR), atmospheric correction (optical), and geometric co-registration, \textbf{28 W-band tiles} (northern Finland, 64--72\textdegree{}N) with complete preprocessing pipelines were retained. The geographic focus on W-band tiles reflects the snow-monitoring objective and the availability of GFSC reference coverage for those tiles; V-band and other tiles were retained in the raw archive but not carried through the full preprocessing chain.

  \item \textbf{Triplet matching.} Constructing training samples required assembling temporally coherent \textit{triplets} of co-located Sentinel-1, Sentinel-2, and GFSC acquisitions. This step is non-trivial: Sentinel-1 has a 6-day revisit cycle, Sentinel-2 a 5-day cycle, and GFSC products are 7-day gap-filled composites with their own composite dating windows. Differences in revisit periods, orbital geometries, and composite dating substantially reduce the number of valid co-temporal combinations. After applying a strict 30-day temporal alignment window, \textbf{29 matched (S1, S2, GFSC) triplets} from \textbf{19 unique W-band MGRS tiles} were obtained and used for training, validation, and testing.
\end{enumerate}

The 29 triplets span \textbf{Dec 2024--July 2025}, covering the transition from snow-free summer conditions through autumn snow onset in northern Lapland, and together represent the full 0--100\% fractional snow cover range in the dataset. GFSC composites are available from the Copernicus Land Monitoring Service HRWSI portal (\url{https://land.copernicus.eu/en/products/snow}). The revised Table~2 (Section~4.1) summarises each sensor's key parameters and archive scope to support direct archive retrieval.

\textit{Changes in manuscript:} Added Table~2 (sensor parameters and archive scope) and revised ``Data Acquisition Details'' paragraph in Section~4.1 with the three-stage provenance description; corrected ``northern Finland winter 2025'' $\rightarrow$ ``northern Finland (December 2024 -- July 2025)'' in Sections~4.4 and~6.

\textbf{R2-5: Formatting inconsistency (10m$\to$60m$\to$10m)}
\begin{quote}
\textit{``Abstract: 10m$\to$60m$\to$10m formatting is slightly inconsistent in the text body.''}
\end{quote}
We thank the reviewer for catching this inconsistency. We have conducted a full formatting sweep across all manuscript sections to standardize the multi-resolution notation. Specifically:

\begin{itemize}
    \item The full three-stage notation now consistently uses $10\text{m} \rightarrow 60\text{m} \rightarrow 10\text{m}$ (with unit inside math mode) throughout the abstract, methodology, discussion, and conclusion sections.
    \item Two-stage references in Section 3.2 sub-headings (``Resolution Aggregation'' and ``Guided Disaggregation'') were corrected from mixed formatting (plain text \texttt{m} outside math mode) to the consistent $\text{m}$ format matching the rest of the manuscript.
    \item Comparison operators with resolution values (e.g., $\geq$60m, $<$60m) were also standardized across all sections.
\end{itemize}

\textit{Changes in manuscript:} Section 3.2 (lines 33, 45: corrected arrow notation formatting); Section 1.1 (line 10: standardized comparison operator format).

\textbf{R2-6: Missing citation ``[? ]''}
\begin{quote}
\textit{``Page 6, line 5 of Section 2.1: `[? ]' indicates a missing citation.''}
\end{quote}
We thank the reviewer for identifying this error. The missing citation was caused by an incorrect bibliography key (\texttt{riggs2017overview}) that did not match any entry in our bibliography file. We have corrected this to reference \citet{riggs2017modis}, which properly cites the VIIRS snow cover product algorithm documentation.

\textit{Change in manuscript:} Section 2.1, Line 8: \replaced{\textbackslash citep\{riggs2017modis\}}{\textbackslash citep\{riggs2017overview\}}

