    90→Our hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture achieves state-of-the-art performance across all metrics. On regression tasks, the model reduces MAE by 38.7\% compared to the best baseline (CNN-Only: 22.46\% $\rightarrow$ Ours: 13.77\%), demonstrating superior continuous fractional snow cover prediction. The 5.93~dB PSNR improvement (14.24~dB $\rightarrow$ 23.65~dB) confirms substantial gains in prediction quality. For classification, our method achieves 94.66\% accuracy and 78.71\% F1-score, outperforming CNN-Only by 5.5 percentage points in accuracy and 23.0 percentage points in F1. These gains validate the effectiveness of: (1) explicit cross-modal attention for capturing S1-S2 complementarity; (2) hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture combining local and global features; (3) multi-resolution processing preserving scale-specific information; (4) quality-aware training with uncertainty quantification.
    91→
    92→\textbf{Baseline Comparisons.} Early Fusion concatenates S1 and S2 features followed by a ResNet encoder-decoder, achieving 25.20\% MAE. This simple concatenation strategy fails to capture complex inter-modal relationships. CNN-Only employs dual-path CNNs with attention-based fusion but no transformer, reaching 22.46\% MAE. While improving over early fusion, CNN-Only lacks global context modeling essential for understanding landscape-scale snow distribution patterns. Transformer-Only uses pure Vision Transformer without CNN feature extraction, yielding 27.05\% MAE. The poor performance indicates that local feature extraction through CNNs is essential for capturing fine-scale spatial patterns. Our hybrid design substantially outperforms all alternatives, demonstrating that synergistic combination of CNN local feature extraction with Transformer global context modeling provides benefits beyond individual components.
    93→
    94→\textbf{Statistical Quality and Spatial Consistency.} Beyond standard error metrics, we assess model calibration through statistical and spatial analyses. Our model demonstrates strong linear fit with Pearson correlation $r=0.784$ ($p<0.001$) and coefficient of determination $R^2=0.588$, indicating 58.8\% explained variance in fractional snow cover predictions. This substantially exceeds baselines: CNN-Only ($r=0.525$, $R^2=0.239$), Early Fusion ($r=0.446$, $R^2=0.026$), and Transformer-Only ($r=0.451$, $R^2=0.111$). Spatial autocorrelation analysis via Moran's I statistic yields $I=0.353$ ($p<0.001$), confirming moderate positive spatial autocorrelation in predictions. This indicates physically realistic spatial patterns where neighboring pixels exhibit correlated predictions, consistent with natural snow cover gradients, rather than random noise. The combination of high correlation, substantial explained variance, and appropriate spatial structure validates our model's reliability for operational snow monitoring applications.
