   171→
   172→\textbf{Incremental Component Analysis.} The baseline configuration using simple concatenation fusion achieves 83.98\% accuracy and 45.86\% F1-score with RMSE of 0.313. Adding cross-attention fusion (+3.52 percentage points accuracy, +12.34 F1) demonstrates that explicit modeling of bidirectional dependencies between SAR and optical modalities significantly improves performance. Incorporating the spatial transformer (+3.70 accuracy, +10.60 F1) further validates that global context modeling is essential for understanding landscape-scale snow distribution patterns. The multi-resolution processing framework (+1.95 accuracy, +5.70 F1) enables effective training on coarse-resolution labels while leveraging fine-scale input information. Finally, quality-aware training with uncertainty quantification (+1.51 accuracy, +4.21 F1) ensures robust learning from heterogeneous satellite data. The cumulative improvement from baseline to full model (83.98\% $\rightarrow$ 94.66\% accuracy) demonstrates that each component provides complementary benefits.
   173→
   174→\textbf{Component Removal Analysis.} Removing cross-attention from the full model causes the largest performance drop (94.66\% $\rightarrow$ 89.50\% accuracy, -5.16 points), confirming it as the most critical component for multi-modal fusion. Removing the transformer reduces accuracy by 3.86 points, indicating its importance for global context. Removing multi-resolution processing decreases accuracy by 1.76 points, while removing quality-aware training reduces it by 0.81 points. \added{Notably, XFuse \textit{without} multi-resolution processing (92.90\%) still outperforms the best baseline (CNN-Only: 89.13\%) by 3.77 percentage points, confirming that the performance gains are attributable to the cross-attention fusion and transformer components rather than the resolution strategy.} These results validate that all components contribute meaningfully to the final performance.
   175→
   176→\textbf{Fusion Strategy Comparison.} Late fusion (ensemble of independent models) achieves only 86.20\% accuracy, significantly underperforming our cross-attention approach (94.66\%). Attention-weighted concatenation improves to 88.90\% but still falls short, demonstrating that explicit bidirectional query-key-value transformations in cross-attention are essential for capturing complex inter-sensor relationships beyond simple weighted averaging.
   177→
   178→\textbf{Architecture Comparison.} Pure CNN architecture achieves 89.13\% accuracy, while pure Transformer reaches only 85.35\%. The hybrid architecture substantially outperforms both (94.66\%), validating our design principle that CNNs and Transformers provide complementary strengths: CNNs for efficient local feature extraction and Transformers for global context modeling.
