\section{Experiments and Results}
\label{sec:result}

\setlength{\tabcolsep}{2pt}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}

\textbf{Dataset and Data Splits.} We train and evaluate our hybrid CNN-Transformer fusion architecture on multi-modal remote sensing data combining Sentinel-1 GRDH SAR (2 polarizations: VV/VH at 10m resolution) and Sentinel-2B/C optical imagery (13 spectral bands unified at 10m from native 10m/20m/60m resolutions) \cite{CopernicusDashboard}. Ground truth labels use Gap-Filled Fractional Snow Cover (GFSC) at 60m resolution \cite{CLMS_SnowCover_2016present}. The dataset comprises 29 spatially distinct Sentinel-1/2 tile acquisitions from northern Finland spanning diverse conditions: snow coverage 0--100\%, varying cloud coverage, and heterogeneous land cover. We employ a geographic split to ensure spatial independence: 70\% training (12 tiles, $\sim$54,000 patches), 15\% validation (3 tiles, $\sim$9,000 patches), and 15\% test (4 tiles, 18,117 patches). The 30-day temporal separation between splits prevents data leakage. Data preprocessing includes SAR radiometric calibration to sigma-naught backscatter, Enhanced Lee Sigma speckle filtering (7$\times$7 window), Range-Doppler terrain correction, and atmospheric correction for optical bands using a hybrid Dark Object Subtraction and MODTRAN-based approach incorporating Rayleigh scattering, aerosol transmission (AOT at 550nm), and water vapor absorption corrections.

\begin{figure}[!h]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{images/data_acquisition_pipeline.png}
    \caption{Sentinel-1/2 satellite data acquisition and preprocessing workflow showing data flow from multi-modal satellite acquisition through preprocessing, quality assessment, and paired temporal matching.}
    \label{fig:data-pipeline}
\end{figure}

\added{\textbf{Data Acquisition Details.} Table~\ref{tab:acquisitions} summarises the satellite datasets and geographic coverage to support reproducibility. All Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 scenes are freely accessible from the ESA Copernicus Data Space Ecosystem}\footnotemark[1]; \added{GFSC composites are available from the Copernicus Land Monitoring Service HRWSI portal}\footnotemark[2]. \added{The raw data archive comprises 178 co-registered Sentinel-1/Sentinel-2 acquisition pairs (407\,GB total) collected from December 2024 to July 2025, spanning 45 MGRS tiles across Finland (60--70.5\textdegree{}N, 20--32\textdegree{}E, UTM zones 33--36) with a maximum Sentinel-2 cloud coverage threshold of 20\% applied at retrieval time. After quality-controlled preprocessing, including SAR radiometric calibration, terrain correction, optical atmospheric correction, and geometric co-registration, 28 W-band (northern Finland, 64--72\textdegree{}N) tiles with complete processing pipelines were retained for training data construction. Assembling the final dataset required temporally matching three independently scheduled data streams: Sentinel-1 (6-day revisit cycle), Sentinel-2 (5-day revisit cycle), and GFSC 7-day composites. Differences in revisit periods, orbital geometries, viewing angles, and composite dating windows substantially constrain the number of valid co-temporal combinations; after applying a strict 30-day temporal window for triplet alignment, 29 matched (S1, S2, GFSC) triplets from 19 unique W-band MGRS tiles were obtained. These triplets fall within the December 2024--July 2025 collection window, covering conditions from snow-free summer through autumn snow onset in northern Lapland, and together encompass the full range of fractional snow cover conditions (0--100\%) present in the dataset.}

\footnotetext[1]{\url{https://dataspace.copernicus.eu}}
\footnotetext[2]{\url{https://land.copernicus.eu/en/products/snow}}

% R2-4: Revised table — archive scope and sensor parameters for reproducibility
\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{\added{Dataset summary by sensor: raw archive size, geographic coverage, and key acquisition parameters. The raw archive of 178 co-registered S1/S2 pairs (407\,GB) was reduced to 29 matched triplets after quality-controlled preprocessing and strict temporal co-registration. GFSC: Copernicus CLMS HRWSI Gap-Filled Fractional Snow Cover, version V102. W-band tiles cover northern Finland (64--72\textdegree{}N).}}
\label{tab:acquisitions}

\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{@{}p{2.2cm}p{1.2cm}X X@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Sensor} & \textbf{Res.} & \textbf{Key parameters} & \textbf{Archive / coverage} \\
\midrule
S-1A IW GRDH & 10\,m & VV/VH dual-pol; ascending; 6-day revisit &
178 scenes, Dec 2024--Jul 2025; 28 W-band tiles \\
\midrule
S-2 MSI L2A & 10--60\,m &
13 bands + SCL/AOT/WVP quality layers; cloud $\leq$20\%; 5-day revisit &
~178 co-reg.\ pairs, 407\,GB; 45 MGRS tiles (60--70.5\textdegree{}N) \\
\midrule
GFSC (CLMS) & 60\,m &
P7D composites; version V102; QC\,+\,QCFLAGS &
~21 W-band tiles; 29 matched (S1,\,S2,\,GFSC) triplets from 19 unique MGRS \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{table*}

\textbf{Implementation Details.} Training is conducted on NVIDIA A30 GPUs (24GB memory) using PyTorch 2.0 with mixed-precision (FP16). The model processes patches extracted at 60m resolution (16$\times$16 pixels covering 960m$\times$960m ground area) with batch size 8. AdamW optimizer with $\beta_1=0.9$, $\beta_2=0.999$, weight decay $5 \times 10^{-2}$ trains for 500 epochs maximum with early stopping (patience=50). Learning rate employs cosine annealing: $\eta_{max}=10^{-4}$ decaying to $\eta_{min}=10^{-6}$. Data augmentation includes random horizontal/vertical flipping (p=0.5), rotation ($\pm 15°$, p=0.3), and Gaussian noise ($\sigma=0.01$, p=0.2). Quality-aware training incorporates cloud masks from Sentinel-2 Scene Classification Layer (SCL), GFSC QC layers, and QCFLAGS to down-weight unreliable pixels through multiplicative loss weighting: $\text{weight} = (\text{confidence}^\alpha) \times (\text{quality\_mask}^\beta)$. Training completed in 34.6 hours with best checkpoint saved at epoch 76 based on validation loss.

\textbf{Metrics.} We report both regression and classification metrics. For continuous fractional snow cover prediction (0--100\%), we use \textit{Mean Absolute Error (MAE)}, \textit{Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)}, and \textit{Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio (PSNR)}. For binary snow/no-snow classification, we threshold predictions at 50\% snow cover and compute \textit{Accuracy}, \textit{Precision}, \textit{Recall}, and \textit{F1-score}. Statistical validation includes Pearson correlation coefficient ($r$), coefficient of determination ($R^2$), and Moran's I spatial autocorrelation statistic. Uncertainty quantification is assessed through Expected Calibration Error (ECE). All metrics are computed on the held-out test set.

\added{\textbf{Evaluation Resolution and Protocol.} We explicitly clarify the resolution at which training and evaluation are conducted. During training, Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 inputs at native 10m resolution are aggregated to 60m through mean pooling over $6 \times 6$ pixel blocks to match the GFSC reference data resolution. The model predicts fractional snow cover at 60m resolution, and \textbf{all quantitative metrics reported in this paper are computed at 60m resolution} against GFSC labels. This approach reflects the fundamental constraint that high-resolution ($<$60m) fractional snow cover ground truth is not widely available. The multi-resolution framework described in Section~\ref{sec:multi_resolution} enables optional inference-time disaggregation from 60m predictions to 10m outputs through guided upsampling that leverages high-resolution input features. However, we emphasize that the 10m outputs represent \textit{spatially enhanced predictions} guided by fine-scale input structure, not independently validated high-resolution products. Qualitative assessment confirms that 10m predictions preserve boundary details consistent with Sentinel-2 optical imagery (see Figure~\ref{fig:qualitative}), but quantitative validation of sub-60m accuracy would require independent high-resolution ground truth from UAV surveys, airborne sensors, or alternative products such as Landsat-derived 30m snow cover, this remains an important direction for future work.}

\subsection{Comparison with Baseline Methods}

