\textbf{Incremental Component Analysis.} The baseline configuration using simple concatenation fusion achieves 83.98\% accuracy and 45.86\% F1-score with RMSE of 0.313. Adding cross-attention fusion (+3.52 percentage points accuracy, +12.34 F1) demonstrates that explicit modeling of bidirectional dependencies between SAR and optical modalities significantly improves performance. Incorporating the spatial transformer (+3.70 accuracy, +10.60 F1) further validates that global context modeling is essential for understanding landscape-scale snow distribution patterns. The multi-resolution processing framework (+1.95 accuracy, +5.70 F1) enables effective training on coarse-resolution labels while leveraging fine-scale input information. Finally, quality-aware training with uncertainty quantification (+1.51 accuracy, +4.21 F1) ensures robust learning from heterogeneous satellite data. The cumulative improvement from baseline to full model (83.98\% $\rightarrow$ 94.66\% accuracy) demonstrates that each component provides complementary benefits.

\textbf{Component Removal Analysis.} Removing cross-attention from the full model causes the largest performance drop (94.66\% $\rightarrow$ 89.50\% accuracy, -5.16 points), confirming it as the most critical component for multi-modal fusion. Removing the transformer reduces accuracy by 3.86 points, indicating its importance for global context. Removing multi-resolution processing decreases accuracy by 1.76 points, while removing quality-aware training reduces it by 0.81 points. \added{Notably, XFuse \textit{without} multi-resolution processing (92.90\%) still outperforms the best baseline (CNN-Only: 89.13\%) by 3.77 percentage points, confirming that the performance gains are attributable to the cross-attention fusion and transformer components rather than the resolution strategy.} These results validate that all components contribute meaningfully to the final performance.

\textbf{Fusion Strategy Comparison.} Late fusion (ensemble of independent models) achieves only 86.20\% accuracy, significantly underperforming our cross-attention approach (94.66\%). Attention-weighted concatenation improves to 88.90\% but still falls short, demonstrating that explicit bidirectional query-key-value transformations in cross-attention are essential for capturing complex inter-sensor relationships beyond simple weighted averaging.

\textbf{Architecture Comparison.} Pure CNN architecture achieves 89.13\% accuracy, while pure Transformer reaches only 85.35\%. The hybrid architecture substantially outperforms both (94.66\%), validating our design principle that CNNs and Transformers provide complementary strengths: CNNs for efficient local feature extraction and Transformers for global context modeling.

\subsection{Advanced Uncertainty Quantification and Calibration Analysis}

\subsubsection{Predictive Uncertainty Assessment and Calibration}

The model's uncertainty quantification framework demonstrates excellent calibration properties, with mean uncertainty of $0.108 \pm 0.017$ (10.8\%) appropriately matching the observed $\sim$10\% error rate (100\% - 89.52\% accuracy during validation). This represents well-calibrated probabilistic predictions where uncertainty estimates accurately reflect prediction reliability. Expected Calibration Error (ECE) analysis yields a value of $0.0252$, substantially below the standard threshold of 0.03 for well-calibrated models, indicating excellent agreement between predicted confidence and actual accuracy. The confidence-at-90\%-accuracy metric of 0.891 further confirms proper calibration, with the model exhibiting 89.1\% confidence when achieving 90\% accuracy. Uncertainty distribution analysis reveals median uncertainty of 0.099 with 95th percentile at 0.207, demonstrating that most predictions maintain high confidence while appropriately flagging uncertain cases based on data quality and surface complexity.

